{:edb/db-before-t 2559 :edb/db-after-t 2560 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535872 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:30:24.334Z" 13194139535872 true] [17592186228902 1100 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" 13194139535872 true] [17592186228902 1100 "Consider the following differential equation\n$y''+y=\\tan{x}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n$\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|$" 13194139535872 false] [17592186228902 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x, \\qquad u_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\tan x\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x, \\qquad y_2(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}(x)y_{2}^{′}(x) - y_{1}^{′}(x)y_{2}(x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x)^{′} - (\\cos x)^{′}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\cos x) - (-\\sin x)(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x \\\\\n&= 1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫(f(x)y_{2}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\tan x\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= \\sin x - \\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫(f(x)y_{1}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\tan x\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= ∫\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\tan x + \\sec x &) + (-\\cos x)(\\sin x) \\\\ =-\\cos x\\ln & \\tan x + \\sec x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= A\\cos x + B\\sin x- \\cos x\\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid _(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" 13194139535872 false] [17592186228902 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" 13194139535872 true] [17592186228903 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" 13194139535872 true] [17592186228903 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?" 13194139535872 false] [17592186228903 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx, \\qquad u_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(xe^{x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(e^{x} + xe^{x}) - (e^{x})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= e^{2x} + xe^{2x} - xe^{2x} \\\\\n&= e^{2x} \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot xe^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&=-18∫x^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-6x^{3} \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot e^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&= 18∫xdx \\\\\n&= 9x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (-6x^{3})e^{x} + (9x^{2})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&=-6x^{3}e^{x} + 9x^{3}e^{x} \\\\\n&= 3x^{3}e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x} + 3x^{3}e^{x}_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}\n$$" 13194139535872 false] [17592186228903 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with